{% extends 'dashboard/help/_topic.html.twig' %}
{% block content %}
<p>catto-mail is a self-hosted mail platform for <strong>client applications</strong> — for example a mailing-list application such as ctnlist. The client application decides who gets which message;
catto-mail does the mail infrastructure work:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Validation</strong> — checking whether addresses look deliverable, with the evidence for each verdict.</li>
<li><strong>Sending</strong> — delivering fully prepared, per-recipient messages through its own mail server, at a controlled pace, with DKIM signatures.</li>
<li><strong>Suppression</strong> — never mailing addresses that hard-bounced, complained or opted out globally, whichever client asks.</li>
<li><strong>Events</strong> — recording what happened to each message: submitted, accepted by the remote server, deferred, bounced, complained, opened, clicked.</li>
<li><strong>Notifications</strong> — telling the client application through signed webhooks and its API.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What catto-mail is not</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Not a mailing-list manager.</strong> Subscribers, lists, campaigns, templates and ordinary unsubscribes belong to the client application. catto-mail receives finished messages.</li>
<li><strong>Not a guarantee of inbox delivery.</strong> It can show that the recipient's mail server accepted a message; whether it landed in the inbox or the spam folder is decided by the receiver and is not visible to the sender.</li>
<li><strong>Not proof of reading.</strong> Opens are recorded when a tracking image is fetched, which privacy proxies and scanners also do, and many readers never load images.</li>
<li><strong>Not consent.</strong> A valid address is evidence that mail can be delivered, never permission to send it.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How you use it</h2>
<ol>
<li>Install it on a server and complete the <a href="{{ path('dashboard_operator_setup') }}">System setup</a> (DNS, certificates, DKIM, tests).</li>
<li>Create a client (for your mailing-list application), give it an API key and a verified sending domain.</li>
<li>The client application validates addresses and sends through the API; you watch the dashboards, bounces, complaints and reputation alerts.</li>
<li>For an old list, upload it as an <a href="{{ path('dashboard_operator_help_topic', {topic: 'uploading-lists'}) }}">address batch</a>, validate it and ask the people for permission again.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Finding your way in the dashboard</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>The round buttons on the left</strong> are the areas: Overview, Mail flow (Delivery, Suppressions, Unmatched DSNs, Webhooks), Clients (Clients, Abuse &amp; reputation, Usage &amp; billing, Address batches), System (System setup, Diagnostics, Audit log) and Access (Users, Roles &amp; permissions), with Help below. A number on a button counts what needs a look there. You see only the areas and pages your permissions allow.</li>
<li><strong>The pills at the top</strong> are the pages of the current area. On an overview they choose the period instead: 24 hours, 7, 30 or 90 days, compared with the period before.</li>
<li><strong>At the top right</strong>: search (an ID, an email address, a domain, an external reference or a name), the delivery mode, the red <em>Stop sending</em> button, what needs attention, and your account.</li>
<li><strong>A client's workspace</strong> (its Overview, Sending, Validation, Settings and Usage) is what the client's own users see; operators open it from the client's page.</li>
<li><strong>Times</strong> are shown in the installation's time zone ({{ time_zone_label() }}, set by <code>SMARTHOST_TIMEZONE</code>), and quotas, usage and billing count its calendar days and months.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}
